-- Pair: product type (two-element tuple), with the canonical EQUALITY of a product —
-- componentwise, provided by `Pair` itself.
--
-- ── NO ORDERING HERE YET — THE OBSTACLE IS GONE, THE TICKET IS WI-877 ───────────
--
-- A pair has a canonical order — lexicographic `fst`-then-`snd`, as in every
-- neighbouring language (Haskell's `Ord (a, b)`, Rust's `Ord for (A, B)`, Python
-- tuples, `std::pair`) — and `Pair` should provide it. It does not YET, but the reason
-- is now only that the ordering is a SEPARATE, unblocked ticket: **WI-877**.
--
-- WHAT USED TO BLOCK IT, and what WI-876 changed. Ordering `Pair` cost SEVEN
-- operations in this file where one would do: `compare` carries the lexicographic
-- logic, and `max`/`min`/`gt`/`gte`/`lt`/`lte` were each one line restating "call
-- `compare`, check the sign". `ordered.anthill` already DERIVED all six from `compare`,
-- but only as LAWS — and a rule is not backing (WI-818) — while the eval builtins for
-- exactly those six sat on the SPEC ops (`Ordered.compare`, `PartialOrd.gt`/`gte`/
-- `lt`/`lte`) and compared host SCALARS only. A builtin on a spec op shadows every
-- carrier, so the derivation was unreachable and `Pair`, the first `Ordered` provider
-- those builtins could not handle, was the first to notice.
--
-- MEASURED at the time: with the six written out the whole surface worked; without them
-- `gt(pair(2, 1), pair(1, 9))` died *"expected Ordered scalars of matching type, got
-- Entity and Entity"* on a program that LOADED CLEAN, and dropping `max`/`min` failed
-- the load outright. It was seven-or-nothing, and six workaround operations in the
-- standard library for a defect that already had a ticket was the wrong trade.
--
-- WI-876 removed it: host implementations are keyed PER CARRIER (`operation_map` in a
-- binding block) instead of on the spec op, so `PartialOrd`/`Ordered` now carry the
-- derivation as real default BODIES. A structural `Ordered` carrier needs `compare`
-- ALONE — see `wi876_operation_mapping_test`, which drives exactly that on a local
-- carrier. WI-877 spends that on `Pair`.
--
-- ── EQUALITY IS COMPONENTWISE ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
--
-- The `requires` clauses do double duty: they CONDITION the provisions and they are the
-- EVIDENCE the `eq` body dispatches through. That is the `Set` precedent —
-- `set.anthill`'s sort-level `requires Eq[T]`, honoured through the `provides` fact
-- rather than op-scoped — applied to a two-parameter carrier. Equality needs no
-- per-carrier boilerplate because `PartialEq.eq`'s builtin ALREADY dispatched to a
-- carrier's own `eq` (`semantic_equal`) — it was the ONE builtin family that did, and
-- that asymmetry is what WI-876 removed: the ordering family now reaches a carrier's
-- own implementation by being KEYED to it rather than by testing its operands.
--
-- THE CHAIN IS `PartialEq`, NOT `Eq`, deliberately: with an `Eq` chain,
-- `Pair[A = Float, B = Int64]` became a LOAD ERROR (MEASURED) — `Float` provides
-- `NonEq`, and WI-835's use-site check refuses a `NonEq` carrier at a parameter whose
-- sort `requires Eq` — i.e. `Pair` stopped being a general PRODUCT. `Set`/`Map`
-- genuinely need `Eq` on keys; a pair of anything is a pair.
--
-- The residue, recorded rather than hidden: a sort has ONE `requires` chain shared by
-- every provision it makes, so `provides Eq[Pair]` rides the `PartialEq` chain and
-- claims LAWFUL equality even where a component has only the partial one. MEASURED:
-- `Set[T = Pair[Float, Int64]]` loads while `Set[T = Float]` is refused — a NaN reaches
-- a lawful-key position inside a pair. (That gap PREDATES these provisions: the old
-- `Pair` provided neither `Eq` nor `NonEq` and was simply *unconstrained*; what changed
-- is that the claim is now positively asserted. `eq.anthill`'s own note calls the
-- congruent `NonEq` derivation for composites a follow-up.) Per-provision conditions —
-- 058 §3.8's `provides Eq[Pair] :- Eq[A], Eq[B]` — are the fix: WI-869.
--
-- ONE MORE MEASURED LIMIT, and it is not this file's defect: a NESTED pair whose FIRST
-- component is a `Pair` and whose second is a primitive
-- (`eq(pair(fst: pair(fst: 1, snd: 2), snd: 7), …)`) dies at eval, the second
-- component's compare dispatching to `Pair.eq`. PRE-EXISTING — reproduced on a purely
-- local carrier with no prelude involvement, and `A = Int64, B = <carrier>` passes —
-- but it is this file that makes it reachable from the standard library. WI-871, and
-- pinned in `wi858_pair_orderings_test` so it is not discovered twice.
enum anthill.prelude.Pair
  import anthill.prelude.{Bool, PartialEq, Eq}

  sort A = ?
  sort B = ?
  requires PartialEq[A]
  requires PartialEq[B]
  entity pair(fst: A, snd: B)

  provides PartialEq[Pair]
  provides Eq[Pair]

  operation fst(p: Pair) -> A
  operation snd(p: Pair) -> B
  -- TODO (WI-324 follow-up): add operation bodies. Both `match p case pair(a, _) -> a`
  -- and `p.fst` currently fail the typer with "expected A, got Term" — the entity-field
  -- sort doesn't propagate through self-typed match/access in concrete enum sorts. The
  -- rules below remain as the de-facto implementation until the typer issue is fixed.
  rule fst(pair(?a, ?b)) <=> ?a
  rule snd(pair(?a, ?b)) <=> ?b

  -- Component compares go through the SEMANTIC spec op `PartialEq.eq`, not through
  -- structural unification, so equality dispatches all the way down — a pair of `Set`s
  -- compares its component by MEMBERSHIP. Each call resolves through the matching
  -- `requires` slot above.
  operation eq(a: Pair, b: Pair) -> Bool =
    match a
      case pair(al, ar) ->
        match b
          case pair(bl, br) ->
            if PartialEq.eq(al, bl) then PartialEq.eq(ar, br) else false
end
